    "title": "El Niño Watch issued with increasing odds of it developing this summer",
    "text": "The phenomenon typically causes warmer and drier conditions throughout the northern U.S., while bringing wet conditions and flooding to the Southeast. SEATTLE — With spring right around the corner, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) has issued an El Niño Watch for later this year. NOAA’s Climate Prediction Center expects a transition from La Niña to neutral conditions over the next month, according to a March 12 discussion. There’s a 55% chance of neutral conditions from May through June and a 62% chance that El Niño will likely emerge from June through August and continue through at least the end of the year. El Niño is a weather phenomenon associated with warmer than normal sea surface temperatures in the Pacific Ocean. During El Niño, trade winds weaken. Warm water is pushed back east, toward the west coast of the Americas. The phenomenon typically causes warmer and drier conditions throughout the northern U.S. In the Gulf Coast and Southeast, these periods are wetter than usual and have increased flooding. However, meteorologists caution that while El Niño loads the dice, it isn’t a guarantee of the weather. The Climate Prediction Center said model forecasts are “relatively less accurate” at this time of year, but current conditions point to increasing odds El Niño will develop. Forecasters say it’s still uncertain how strong El Niño conditions could be. There’s currently a one-in-three chance that it will be a strong El Niño from October through December. If El Niño does develop, it would be the first time the nation has been under the influence of El Niño since the 2023-24 winter season. El Niños typically occur about every two to seven years, according to NOAA.",
